## Proof

The equivalence relation is defined by $(x, y, z) \sim (x, y', z')$ for any $y', z' \in \mathbb{R}$. Equivalently, two points are related if and only if they share the same first coordinate $x$. Thus each equivalence class is the entire plane $\{x\} \times \mathbb{R}^2 \subset \mathbb{R}^3$, and the quotient space $\mathbb{R}^3/{\sim}$ is the set of all such planes, indexed by $x \in \mathbb{R}$.

**Claim.** $\mathbb{R}^3/{\sim} \cong \mathbb{R}$ (homeomorphic).

Let $q : \mathbb{R}^3 \to \mathbb{R}^3/{\sim}$ be the quotient map. Define
$$\pi : \mathbb{R}^3 \to \mathbb{R}, \qquad \pi(x, y, z) = x.$$
This map is continuous (it is the projection onto the first coordinate). Since $\pi$ is constant on each equivalence class (it depends only on $x$), the universal property of the quotient topology yields a unique continuous map
$$\widetilde{\pi} : \mathbb{R}^3/{\sim} \to \mathbb{R}$$
such that $\pi = \widetilde{\pi} \circ q$.

**$\widetilde{\pi}$ is bijective.** It is surjective because for every $x \in \mathbb{R}$, the class $[(x, 0, 0)]$ maps to $x$. It is injective because if $\widetilde{\pi}([(x_1, y_1, z_1)]) = \widetilde{\pi}([(x_2, y_2, z_2)])$, then $x_1 = x_2$, which means $(x_1, y_1, z_1) \sim (x_2, y_2, z_2)$, so the two classes are equal.

**$\widetilde{\pi}$ is a homeomorphism.** It remains to show $\widetilde{\pi}$ is open (equivalently, that its inverse is continuous). A subset $U \subseteq \mathbb{R}^3/{\sim}$ is open (by definition of the quotient topology) if and only if $q^{-1}(U)$ is open in $\mathbb{R}^3$. Now $q^{-1}(U) = \pi^{-1}(\widetilde{\pi}(U))$, since a point $(x,y,z)$ lies in $q^{-1}(U)$ exactly when its class lies in $U$, i.e. when $x \in \widetilde{\pi}(U)$. The set $q^{-1}(U) = \pi^{-1}(\widetilde{\pi}(U)) = \widetilde{\pi}(U) \times \mathbb{R}^2$ is open in $\mathbb{R}^3$ if and only if $\widetilde{\pi}(U)$ is open in $\mathbb{R}$ (a product $A \times \mathbb{R}^2$ is open in $\mathbb{R}^3$ iff $A$ is open in $\mathbb{R}$, since $\mathbb{R}^2$ is open in itself). Therefore $U$ is open in the quotient if and only if $\widetilde{\pi}(U)$ is open in $\mathbb{R}$, proving $\widetilde{\pi}$ is an open map.

Hence $\widetilde{\pi}$ is a continuous bijection that is also open, so it is a homeomorphism. Therefore

$$\mathbb{R}^3/{\sim} \cong \mathbb{R}.$$

